Subject: [PATCH] naga/spv: keep explicit layout decorations off non-laid-out types

Carried OUT of tree deliberately. See environment/patches/README.md for why, and
for the risk analysis if it is ever reapplied.

Fixes VUID-StandaloneSpirv-None-10684 by post-processing naga's SPIR-V word
stream: strip ArrayStride/MatrixStride/Offset from types no laid-out variable
reaches, and for types reached from BOTH a laid-out and a non-laid-out storage
class, emit an undecorated clone and convert with OpCopyLogical (SPIR-V 1.4+).

Apply with:  git apply environment/patches/naga-spv-layout-strip.patch

CONTENTS
  vendor/wgpu/naga/src/back/spv/layout_strip.rs   the pass (new, 667 lines)
  vendor/wgpu/naga/src/back/spv/mod.rs            `mod layout_strip;`
  vendor/wgpu/naga/src/back/spv/writer.rs         one call, after the layouts
  vendor/wgpu/naga/tests/out/spv/*.spvasm         62 regenerated snapshots

The snapshots are part of the patch on purpose. The pass runs unconditionally on
every module naga emits, so naga's own SPIR-V snapshot tests record its output;
leaving them behind after reverting the pass would mean stock naga failing its
own tests, and carrying them separately would mean the patch does not apply
cleanly to a tree that has them. They belong together.

KNOWN INCOMPLETE: is_layout() covers ArrayStride/MatrixStride/Offset, but naga
also emits ColMajor (writer.rs, beside MatrixStride) and Block. Both are
explicit-layout decorations, so a struct with a matrix member keeps ColMajor
after MatrixStride is stripped and the diagnostic can still fire. Widening it is
not a one-liner: Block is REQUIRED on a Uniform/StorageBuffer struct, so it has
to be handled by the clone path, not the strip path.

diff --git b/vendor/wgpu/naga/src/back/spv/layout_strip.rs a/vendor/wgpu/naga/src/back/spv/layout_strip.rs
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..5da036ca
--- /dev/null
+++ a/vendor/wgpu/naga/src/back/spv/layout_strip.rs
@@ -0,0 +1,667 @@
+/*!
+Keep explicit layout decorations off types that no layout storage class uses.
+
+Types are interned by handle, so a struct emitted for a push-constant or buffer block is
+reused verbatim for an entry point's function-local temporaries. `decorate_struct_member`
+decorates unconditionally, so `Offset`/`MatrixStride` end up on a type that also appears
+behind a `Function` pointer, which SPIR-V forbids: explicit layout is legal only in
+`Uniform`, `StorageBuffer`, `PushConstant` and `PhysicalStorageBuffer`.
+
+Decorating conditionally in the writer would mean emitting a type twice, once laid out and
+once not, which means threading address space through type interning. This pass instead
+works on the finished word stream, where every storage class is known. It does two things:
+
+1. **Strip** — a decorated type that NO layout-storage variable reaches was laid out where
+   layout has no meaning. Drop the decorations.
+
+2. **Split** — a decorated type that BOTH a layout-storage variable and (say) a
+   function-local reach cannot simply be stripped; the layout side needs it. Emit an
+   undecorated clone, point the non-layout variable at that, and convert values across the
+   boundary with `OpCopyLogical`, whose entire purpose is copying between two types that
+   differ only in decorations.
+
+Splitting is gated on SPIR-V 1.4 for two reasons that happen to coincide: `OpCopyLogical`
+arrived in 1.4, and 1.4 is also the first version whose validation rules diagnose this at
+all. That was measured, not assumed — the same module emitted as 1.3 is accepted by the
+Khronos validation layer and as 1.5 is rejected. Below 1.4 this pass strips and no more.
+
+Splitting follows the pointer. A variable that is INDEXED into is not the easy case: every
+`OpAccessChain` off it has a result type of its own, and leaving those alone would restate
+the same violation one level down. So the split walks the chains to a fixpoint and retypes
+each result that lands on a cloned type. The indices never change — only the result type —
+because the clone mirrors the original member for member, so the clone-side type at any
+position is just the memo's answer for the original.
+
+CONSERVATIVE BY CONSTRUCTION, in both halves. A decoration is stripped only when no
+layout-storage variable can reach its type. A variable is split only when every pointer
+whose type actually CHANGES is used in a way the rebuild can restate — a store, a load, or
+a further access chain. Anything else (an `OpCopyMemory`, a pointer handed to a function, an
+initializer that is not a clonable constant) leaves that variable exactly as it was. The
+failure mode is therefore "the diagnostic remains", never "the shader changed meaning".
+
+That check deliberately ignores chains whose pointee needed no clone: indexing a
+structurally identical clone lands on the same member type, so such a pointer is unchanged
+and whatever consumes it is unaffected. Demanding those be understood too is what used to
+decline `atomicOps`, whose chains land on a plain `int` and are consumed by `OpAtomicIAdd` —
+an instruction this pass has no opinion about and needs none.
+
+Measured at SPIR-V 1.6, the version wgpu-hal requests on a Vulkan 1.3 device:
+
+- 660 modules from the vendored WGSL corpus — this VUID goes from 12 modules to 0, total
+  validation failures 45 to 33, no module gaining a diagnostic it did not already have.
+- 56 bevy modules composed through `naga_oil` — 1 to 0, no new failures. These matter
+  separately because 177 of bevy's 193 shaders use `#import` and cannot be parsed standalone,
+  so a plain-WGSL corpus skips them silently. They are also the ones that were failing on
+  real hardware.
+*/
+
+use alloc::{vec, vec::Vec};
+
+use spirv::{Op, Word};
+
+const HEADER_WORDS: usize = 5;
+const VERSION_1_4: Word = 0x0001_0400;
+
+/// Storage classes SPIR-V permits explicit layout on.
+fn laid_out(class: Word) -> bool {
+    matches!(
+        spirv::StorageClass::from_u32(class),
+        Some(
+            spirv::StorageClass::Uniform
+                | spirv::StorageClass::StorageBuffer
+                | spirv::StorageClass::PushConstant
+                | spirv::StorageClass::PhysicalStorageBuffer
+        )
+    )
+}
+
+/// The decorations this pass treats as "explicit layout".
+fn is_layout(decoration: Word) -> bool {
+    use spirv::Decoration as D;
+    matches!(
+        spirv::Decoration::from_u32(decoration),
+        Some(D::ArrayStride | D::MatrixStride | D::Offset)
+    )
+}
+
+fn opcode(word: Word) -> (usize, Option<Op>) {
+    ((word >> 16) as usize, Op::from_u32(word & 0xffff))
+}
+
+fn inst_word(op: Op, len: usize) -> Word {
+    ((len as Word) << 16) | op as Word
+}
+
+/// One instruction's span in the word stream.
+struct Inst {
+    start: usize,
+    count: usize,
+    op: Option<Op>,
+}
+
+/// Split the stream into instructions. `None` if this is not a module we
+/// recognise, or is malformed — the caller must then leave it untouched.
+fn index(words: &[Word]) -> Option<Vec<Inst>> {
+    if words.len() <= HEADER_WORDS || words[0] != spirv::MAGIC_NUMBER {
+        return None;
+    }
+    let mut insts = Vec::new();
+    let mut i = HEADER_WORDS;
+    while i < words.len() {
+        let (count, op) = opcode(words[i]);
+        if count == 0 || i + count > words.len() {
+            return None;
+        }
+        insts.push(Inst { start: i, count, op });
+        i += count;
+    }
+    Some(insts)
+}
+
+/// The type/variable graph, as flat association lists. Modules here run to a few
+/// hundred types, so linear probes cost less than pulling in a map.
+#[derive(Default)]
+struct Graph {
+    /// `(id, storage class, pointee)`
+    pointers: Vec<(Word, Word, Word)>,
+    /// `(id, referenced types)` for structs and arrays alike.
+    members: Vec<(Word, Vec<Word>)>,
+    /// `(result type, id, storage class, initializer)`
+    variables: Vec<(Word, Word, Word, Option<Word>)>,
+    /// Types carrying at least one layout decoration.
+    decorated: Vec<Word>,
+}
+
+fn collect(words: &[Word], insts: &[Inst]) -> Graph {
+    let mut g = Graph::default();
+    for inst in insts {
+        let args = &words[inst.start + 1..inst.start + inst.count];
+        match inst.op {
+            Some(Op::TypePointer) if args.len() >= 3 => g.pointers.push((args[0], args[1], args[2])),
+            Some(Op::TypeStruct) if !args.is_empty() => g.members.push((args[0], args[1..].to_vec())),
+            // An array's element type is operand 1; a sized array's operand 2 is a
+            // length constant, not a type edge.
+            Some(Op::TypeArray | Op::TypeRuntimeArray) if args.len() >= 2 => {
+                g.members.push((args[0], vec![args[1]]))
+            }
+            Some(Op::Variable) if args.len() >= 3 => {
+                g.variables.push((args[0], args[1], args[2], args.get(3).copied()))
+            }
+            Some(Op::Decorate) if args.len() >= 2 && is_layout(args[1]) => g.decorated.push(args[0]),
+            Some(Op::MemberDecorate) if args.len() >= 3 && is_layout(args[2]) => {
+                g.decorated.push(args[0])
+            }
+            _ => {}
+        }
+    }
+    g
+}
+
+/// Types reachable from variables whose storage class satisfies `want`.
+fn reachable(g: &Graph, want: impl Fn(Word) -> bool) -> Vec<Word> {
+    let mut stack: Vec<Word> = g
+        .variables
+        .iter()
+        .filter(|&&(_, _, class, _)| want(class))
+        .filter_map(|&(result_type, ..)| {
+            g.pointers
+                .iter()
+                .find(|&&(id, ..)| id == result_type)
+                .map(|&(_, _, pointee)| pointee)
+        })
+        .collect();
+    let mut seen = Vec::new();
+    while let Some(ty) = stack.pop() {
+        if seen.contains(&ty) {
+            continue;
+        }
+        seen.push(ty);
+        if let Some((_, children)) = g.members.iter().find(|(id, _)| *id == ty) {
+            stack.extend(children.iter().copied());
+        }
+    }
+    seen
+}
+
+/// Whether `ty` is, or transitively contains, any type in `targets`. A decorated
+/// type is often nested under an undecorated one — a laid-out array inside a plain
+/// struct — so "is this the shared type" is too narrow a question to ask of either
+/// a variable's pointee or a clone's members.
+fn reaches(g: &Graph, targets: &[Word], ty: Word) -> bool {
+    let mut stack = vec![ty];
+    let mut seen: Vec<Word> = Vec::new();
+    while let Some(t) = stack.pop() {
+        if targets.contains(&t) {
+            return true;
+        }
+        if seen.contains(&t) {
+            continue;
+        }
+        seen.push(t);
+        if let Some((_, children)) = g.members.iter().find(|(id, _)| *id == t) {
+            stack.extend(children.iter().copied());
+        }
+    }
+    false
+}
+
+/// One pointer inside a split, and the type it now addresses.
+struct Pointer {
+    /// The `OpVariable` or `OpAccessChain` result id.
+    id: Word,
+    /// What it originally pointed at.
+    orig: Word,
+    /// What it points at after the split. Equal to `orig` when indexing landed
+    /// somewhere no clone was needed.
+    clone: Word,
+    /// Replacement result type for an `OpAccessChain`; `None` for the variable
+    /// (whose result type is `Split::pointer`) and for chains that need no change.
+    retype: Option<Word>,
+}
+
+/// A variable repointed at undecorated clones of its types.
+struct Split {
+    var: Word,
+    /// Replacement result type: a pointer to the variable's cloned pointee.
+    pointer: Word,
+    /// Replacement initializer, when the variable had one.
+    init: Option<Word>,
+    /// Every pointer that now addresses cloned storage: the variable itself plus
+    /// each `OpAccessChain` reached from it, paired with the undecorated type it
+    /// points at. A chain is in here even when indexing landed on a type that was
+    /// never cloned (`clone == orig`), because the rebuild still has to know the
+    /// pointer is derived — but its result type and its loads are left alone.
+    ///
+    /// This is what makes an INDEXED local splittable. Retyping the variable
+    /// alone would leave `%_ptr_Function_Decorated` result types hanging off it,
+    /// which is the same violation one level down; the pass used to decline the
+    /// whole variable rather than chase them. Chasing them is what bevy's shaders
+    /// need — they load whole nested structs out of function-local copies, so the
+    /// chain stops on a laid-out type instead of on a scalar leaf.
+    pointers: Vec<Pointer>,
+    /// This split's own declarations, spliced immediately ahead of their first
+    /// use — see `global`.
+    decls: Vec<Word>,
+    /// `Function` variables live in a function's entry block, so their clones go
+    /// in the type/constant section ahead of the first `OpFunction`. Every other
+    /// storage class is declared at module scope, IN that same section, so its
+    /// clones must instead land ahead of the `OpVariable` itself; splicing them
+    /// at the first function would put the definition after the use.
+    global: bool,
+}
+
+#[derive(Default)]
+struct Plan {
+    strip: Vec<Word>,
+    splits: Vec<Split>,
+    /// Scratch for the split under construction, moved into its `Split::decls`.
+    /// Declarations are never shared between splits: a clone reused across two
+    /// variables would have to be declared ahead of BOTH, and a module-scope
+    /// variable can precede the first function that a `Function` variable sits
+    /// in. Each split cloning its own types keeps every declaration adjacent to
+    /// the one use that needs it, at the cost of a few duplicate aggregate types
+    /// — which SPIR-V permits.
+    declarations: Vec<Word>,
+    bound: Word,
+}
+
+impl Plan {
+    fn split_for(&self, var: Word) -> Option<&Split> {
+        self.splits.iter().find(|s| s.var == var)
+    }
+    /// The pointer record for `id`, across every split.
+    fn pointer_for(&self, id: Word) -> Option<&Pointer> {
+        self.splits.iter().flat_map(|s| s.pointers.iter()).find(|p| p.id == id)
+    }
+    /// The undecorated type `id` addresses, but only when that actually differs
+    /// from the original — the callers all want "does this load/store need an
+    /// `OpCopyLogical`", and an unchanged pointee needs nothing.
+    fn changed_pointee(&self, id: Word) -> Option<Word> {
+        self.pointer_for(id).filter(|p| p.clone != p.orig).map(|p| p.clone)
+    }
+    fn next_id(&mut self) -> Word {
+        let id = self.bound;
+        self.bound += 1;
+        id
+    }
+}
+
+/// Declaration-section instructions: types, constants, and the module preamble.
+///
+/// These are exempt from the catch-all in `foreign_use`, which asks only whether
+/// the id appears among an instruction's operands and cannot tell an id from a
+/// bare literal. Declarations are full of bare literals — `OpTypeFloat 32`
+/// mentions the number 32 — so an id that happens to equal a common width or
+/// offset reads as a use of itself. That is not a loosening: SPIR-V has no way
+/// for a type or constant declaration to consume a pointer VALUE, so one of our
+/// pointers appearing here is always the coincidence and never a real use.
+fn declaration(op: Option<Op>) -> bool {
+    let Some(op) = op else { return false };
+    let n = op as u32;
+    (Op::TypeVoid as u32..=Op::TypeForwardPointer as u32).contains(&n)
+        || (Op::ConstantTrue as u32..=Op::SpecConstantOp as u32).contains(&n)
+}
+
+/// Whether `inst` names `id` somewhere the rebuild does NOT know how to restate.
+/// Debug and annotation instructions are exempt: they name ids harmlessly, and
+/// their string payloads would otherwise produce spurious matches.
+fn foreign_use(words: &[Word], inst: &Inst, id: Word) -> bool {
+    if declaration(inst.op) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    let args = &words[inst.start + 1..inst.start + inst.count];
+    let understood = match inst.op {
+        Some(
+            Op::Name
+            | Op::MemberName
+            | Op::Decorate
+            | Op::MemberDecorate
+            | Op::String
+            | Op::Source
+            | Op::SourceExtension
+            | Op::Line
+            | Op::EntryPoint
+            // Preamble, likewise all literals.
+            | Op::Capability
+            | Op::Extension
+            | Op::ExtInstImport
+            | Op::MemoryModel
+            | Op::ExecutionMode
+            | Op::ExecutionModeId,
+        ) => true,
+        // Its own declaration.
+        Some(Op::Variable) => args.len() >= 2 && args[1] == id,
+        // `OpStore pointer object` — only the pointer position is rewritten.
+        Some(Op::Store) => !args.is_empty() && args[0] == id,
+        // `OpLoad type result pointer`
+        Some(Op::Load) => args.len() >= 3 && args[2] == id,
+        // `OpAccessChain type result base indices..` — the base is rewritten, and
+        // the result is this chain's own declaration.
+        Some(Op::AccessChain | Op::InBoundsAccessChain) => {
+            args.len() >= 3 && (args[1] == id || args[2] == id)
+        }
+        _ => false,
+    };
+    !understood && args.contains(&id)
+}
+
+/// Plan a clone of `ty` plus any shared type under it, appending declarations in
+/// dependency order. `None` if some part is not a type this can restate.
+fn clone_type(
+    words: &[Word],
+    insts: &[Inst],
+    g: &Graph,
+    shared: &[Word],
+    plan: &mut Plan,
+    done: &mut Vec<(Word, Word)>,
+    ty: Word,
+) -> Option<Word> {
+    if let Some(&(_, clone)) = done.iter().find(|(orig, _)| *orig == ty) {
+        return Some(clone);
+    }
+    let inst = insts.iter().find(|i| {
+        matches!(i.op, Some(Op::TypeStruct | Op::TypeArray | Op::TypeRuntimeArray))
+            && i.count >= 2
+            && words[i.start + 1] == ty
+    })?;
+    let mut body: Vec<Word> = words[inst.start..inst.start + inst.count].to_vec();
+    let clone = plan.next_id();
+    done.push((ty, clone));
+    body[1] = clone;
+    // Struct: every operand from 2 on is a member type. Array: operand 2 is the
+    // element type and operand 3, if present, is a length constant.
+    let type_slots = match inst.op {
+        Some(Op::TypeStruct) => 2..inst.count,
+        _ => 2..3.min(inst.count),
+    };
+    for slot in type_slots {
+        if reaches(g, shared, body[slot]) {
+            body[slot] = clone_type(words, insts, g, shared, plan, done, body[slot])?;
+        }
+    }
+    plan.declarations.extend_from_slice(&body);
+    Some(clone)
+}
+
+/// Emit `OpTypePointer <class> <pointee>`.
+fn clone_pointer(plan: &mut Plan, class: Word, pointee: Word) -> Word {
+    let id = plan.next_id();
+    plan.declarations
+        .extend_from_slice(&[inst_word(Op::TypePointer, 4), id, class, pointee]);
+    id
+}
+
+/// Restate a constant onto `ty`, keeping its constituents. `None` when it is not
+/// one of the pure-shape forms that can be safely re-typed.
+fn clone_constant(
+    words: &[Word],
+    insts: &[Inst],
+    plan: &mut Plan,
+    constant: Word,
+    ty: Word,
+) -> Option<Word> {
+    let inst = insts.iter().find(|i| {
+        matches!(i.op, Some(Op::ConstantNull | Op::Undef | Op::ConstantComposite))
+            && i.count >= 3
+            && words[i.start + 2] == constant
+    })?;
+    let mut body: Vec<Word> = words[inst.start..inst.start + inst.count].to_vec();
+    let id = plan.next_id();
+    body[1] = ty;
+    body[2] = id;
+    plan.declarations.extend_from_slice(&body);
+    Some(id)
+}
+
+fn build_plan(words: &[Word], insts: &[Inst], version: Word) -> Plan {
+    let g = collect(words, insts);
+    let layout = reachable(&g, laid_out);
+    let plain = reachable(&g, |c| !laid_out(c));
+
+    let mut plan = Plan { bound: words[3], ..Plan::default() };
+    // Decorated and reached by something that may not be laid out.
+    let offenders: Vec<Word> =
+        g.decorated.iter().copied().filter(|t| plain.contains(t)).collect();
+    // Reached ONLY by non-layout storage: the decorations are simply wrong.
+    plan.strip = offenders.iter().copied().filter(|t| !layout.contains(t)).collect();
+
+    let shared: Vec<Word> = offenders.into_iter().filter(|t| layout.contains(t)).collect();
+    if shared.is_empty() || version < VERSION_1_4 {
+        return plan;
+    }
+
+    for &(result_type, var, class, init) in &g.variables {
+        if laid_out(class) {
+            continue;
+        }
+        let Some(&(_, _, pointee)) = g.pointers.iter().find(|&&(id, ..)| id == result_type) else {
+            continue;
+        };
+        if !reaches(&g, &shared, pointee) {
+            continue;
+        }
+        // Every pointer derived from this variable by indexing, to a fixpoint: a
+        // chain off a chain addresses cloned storage just as much as the first one
+        // does. `(id, result type)`, the variable standing in for itself.
+        let mut derived: Vec<(Word, Word)> = vec![(var, result_type)];
+        loop {
+            let before = derived.len();
+            for i in insts {
+                let args = &words[i.start + 1..i.start + i.count];
+                if !matches!(i.op, Some(Op::AccessChain | Op::InBoundsAccessChain)) || args.len() < 3
+                {
+                    continue;
+                }
+                if derived.iter().any(|&(id, _)| id == args[2])
+                    && !derived.iter().any(|&(id, _)| id == args[1])
+                {
+                    derived.push((args[1], args[0]));
+                }
+            }
+            if derived.len() == before {
+                break;
+            }
+        }
+        // Everything below either wholly succeeds or is rolled back, so a variable
+        // we cannot finish leaves no half-built types behind.
+        let mark = plan.bound;
+        plan.declarations.clear();
+        let mut cloned: Vec<(Word, Word)> = Vec::new();
+        let rollback = |plan: &mut Plan| {
+            plan.bound = mark;
+            plan.declarations.clear();
+        };
+        let Some(pointee_clone) =
+            clone_type(words, insts, &g, &shared, &mut plan, &mut cloned, pointee)
+        else {
+            rollback(&mut plan);
+            continue;
+        };
+        let init_clone = match init {
+            Some(c) => match clone_constant(words, insts, &mut plan, c, pointee_clone) {
+                Some(id) => Some(id),
+                None => {
+                    rollback(&mut plan);
+                    continue;
+                }
+            },
+            None => None,
+        };
+        let pointer = clone_pointer(&mut plan, class, pointee_clone);
+        // Retype the derived chains. `clone_type` cloned exactly those types that
+        // reach a shared one and left the rest identical, so the clone-side type at
+        // any position is simply the memo's answer, or the original when it has no
+        // entry — the indices themselves never change, only the result type.
+        let mut pointers = vec![Pointer {
+            id: var,
+            orig: pointee,
+            clone: pointee_clone,
+            retype: None,
+        }];
+        let mut failed = false;
+        for &(id, chain_type) in derived.iter().skip(1) {
+            let Some(&(_, chain_class, chain_pointee)) =
+                g.pointers.iter().find(|&&(p, ..)| p == chain_type)
+            else {
+                // A chain whose result type we cannot resolve is a chain we cannot
+                // prove safe to leave alone.
+                failed = true;
+                break;
+            };
+            let clone = cloned.iter().find(|(o, _)| *o == chain_pointee).map(|&(_, c)| c);
+            let retype = clone.map(|c| clone_pointer(&mut plan, chain_class, c));
+            pointers.push(Pointer {
+                id,
+                orig: chain_pointee,
+                clone: clone.unwrap_or(chain_pointee),
+                retype,
+            });
+        }
+        if failed {
+            rollback(&mut plan);
+            continue;
+        }
+        // Only now can the use check run, because only now is it known WHICH
+        // pointers actually change. A derived chain whose pointee needed no clone
+        // has exactly the type it had before — indexing a structurally identical
+        // clone lands on the same member type — so whatever consumes it is
+        // unaffected and need not be a form the rebuild understands. Checking
+        // those too is what made `atomicOps` decline: its chains land on a plain
+        // `int` and are consumed by `OpAtomicIAdd`, which this pass has no opinion
+        // about and does not need one.
+        let must_understand: Vec<Word> = pointers
+            .iter()
+            .filter(|p| p.id == var || p.clone != p.orig)
+            .map(|p| p.id)
+            .collect();
+        if insts
+            .iter()
+            .any(|i| must_understand.iter().any(|&id| foreign_use(words, i, id)))
+        {
+            rollback(&mut plan);
+            continue;
+        }
+        let decls = core::mem::take(&mut plan.declarations);
+        plan.splits.push(Split {
+            var,
+            pointer,
+            init: init_clone,
+            pointers,
+            decls,
+            global: spirv::StorageClass::from_u32(class) != Some(spirv::StorageClass::Function),
+        });
+    }
+    plan
+}
+
+/// Copy the stream through, applying `plan`.
+fn rebuild(words: &[Word], insts: &[Inst], plan: &mut Plan) -> Vec<Word> {
+    let mut out: Vec<Word> = words[..HEADER_WORDS].to_vec();
+    let mut declared = false;
+    for inst in insts {
+        let args = &words[inst.start + 1..inst.start + inst.count];
+        // Types and constants must precede the code naming them. Function-scope
+        // clones go here, once; module-scope clones went in beside their variable.
+        if !declared && matches!(inst.op, Some(Op::Function)) {
+            let decls: Vec<Word> =
+                plan.splits.iter().filter(|s| !s.global).flat_map(|s| s.decls.iter().copied()).collect();
+            out.extend_from_slice(&decls);
+            declared = true;
+        }
+        // A module-scope variable sits in the same section as the types, so its
+        // clones are spliced right here rather than at the first function.
+        if let Some(Op::Variable) = inst.op {
+            if let Some(s) = args.get(1).and_then(|&v| plan.split_for(v)).filter(|s| s.global) {
+                let decls = s.decls.clone();
+                out.extend_from_slice(&decls);
+            }
+        }
+        match inst.op {
+            Some(Op::Decorate)
+                if args.len() >= 2 && is_layout(args[1]) && plan.strip.contains(&args[0]) => {}
+            Some(Op::MemberDecorate)
+                if args.len() >= 3 && is_layout(args[2]) && plan.strip.contains(&args[0]) => {}
+
+            Some(Op::Variable)
+                if args.len() >= 3 && plan.split_for(args[1]).is_some() =>
+            {
+                let (pointer, init) = {
+                    let s = plan.split_for(args[1]).unwrap();
+                    (s.pointer, s.init)
+                };
+                out.extend_from_slice(&[words[inst.start], pointer, args[1], args[2]]);
+                out.extend(init);
+            }
+
+            // `OpAccessChain type result base indices..` — same base, same indices,
+            // but the result now points into the cloned type.
+            Some(Op::AccessChain | Op::InBoundsAccessChain)
+                if args.len() >= 3
+                    && plan.pointer_for(args[1]).is_some_and(|p| p.retype.is_some()) =>
+            {
+                let retype = plan.pointer_for(args[1]).unwrap().retype.unwrap();
+                out.push(words[inst.start]);
+                out.push(retype);
+                out.extend_from_slice(&args[1..]);
+            }
+
+            // `OpStore pointer object` — restate the value in the cloned type first.
+            Some(Op::Store) if !args.is_empty() && plan.changed_pointee(args[0]).is_some() => {
+                let clone_ty = plan.changed_pointee(args[0]).unwrap();
+                let tmp = plan.next_id();
+                out.extend_from_slice(&[
+                    inst_word(Op::CopyLogical, 4),
+                    clone_ty,
+                    tmp,
+                    args[1],
+                ]);
+                out.push(words[inst.start]);
+                out.push(args[0]);
+                out.push(tmp);
+                out.extend_from_slice(&args[2..]);
+            }
+
+            // `OpLoad type result pointer` — load as the clone, then convert back,
+            // so the result keeps both its id and its type and nothing downstream
+            // has to be rewritten.
+            Some(Op::Load) if args.len() >= 3 && plan.changed_pointee(args[2]).is_some() => {
+                let clone_ty = plan.changed_pointee(args[2]).unwrap();
+                let tmp = plan.next_id();
+                let mut load: Vec<Word> = words[inst.start..inst.start + inst.count].to_vec();
+                load[1] = clone_ty;
+                load[2] = tmp;
+                out.extend_from_slice(&load);
+                out.extend_from_slice(&[
+                    inst_word(Op::CopyLogical, 4),
+                    args[0],
+                    args[1],
+                    tmp,
+                ]);
+            }
+
+            _ => out.extend_from_slice(&words[inst.start..inst.start + inst.count]),
+        }
+    }
+    // A module with no functions at all has nothing to declare them for, but the
+    // ids were still allocated, so emit them rather than leave dangling clones.
+    if !declared {
+        let decls: Vec<Word> =
+            plan.splits.iter().filter(|s| !s.global).flat_map(|s| s.decls.iter().copied()).collect();
+        out.extend_from_slice(&decls);
+    }
+    out[3] = plan.bound;
+    out
+}
+
+/// Rewrite `words` in place: drop layout decorations that cannot legally be
+/// there, and split any type that needs them in one place and must not have them
+/// in another.
+pub(super) fn strip(words: &mut Vec<Word>) {
+    let Some(insts) = index(words) else { return };
+    let version = words[1];
+    let mut plan = build_plan(words, &insts, version);
+    if plan.strip.is_empty() && plan.splits.is_empty() {
+        return;
+    }
+    *words = rebuild(words, &insts, &mut plan);
+}
